Chronic Illness / Disability Identity Grief - Prolonged Grief Disorder (or Adjustment Disorder) - V62.89 (Phase of life problem)
Diversity Equity and Inclusion
Loss of physical capacity grief, Somatic betrayal, Assistive device reluctance

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: DEI-005
Reason for Visit: Acute physical collapse and profound emotional distress following extreme over-exertion, necessitating urgent assessment to rule out physiological decompensation.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported a severe post-exertional crash accompanied by profound weakness, tachycardia, and intense existential despair, requiring immediate assessment to exclude acute cardiac event or metabolic crisis prior to the next available routine GP appointment.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I pushed myself to prove I'm still normal, and now my body has completely failed me and I can barely move.
History of Present Illness: The patient describes a history of oscillating periods of profound sadness regarding the loss of former physical capacity. Earlier today, the patient engaged in extreme over-exertion in an attempt to prove physical normality to able-bodied peers, which was followed by a severe, acute post-exertional crash. They report intense anger directed at the unpredictable nature of the illness and persistent feelings of betrayed trust directed toward their own body (somatic betrayal). The spouse notes the patient's reluctance to adopt necessary assistive devices due to internalized stigma directly contributed to today's collapse. The patient expresses deep existential questioning regarding personal worth, chronic fear of disease progression, and isolation from former friend groups centered around physical activities.
Functional Status: Normally mobilizes with difficulty, but currently bedbound due to severe acute exhaustion.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of falls due to refusal to use mobility aids and physical exhaustion.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 95/60 | HR: 108 | Temp: 36.4 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 96% | Wt: 68 Kg
General: Appears pale, profoundly exhausted, lying flat in bed, and tearful.
Physical Exam: Cardiovascular: Tachycardia, weak peripheral pulses, no murmurs. Respiratory: Clear breath sounds, unlabored breathing. Musculoskeletal: Generalized profound muscle weakness; unable to straight-leg raise against gravity. Neurological: Alert and oriented, but speech is slow and fatigued. Cranial nerves intact. Deep loss of physical capacity grief and somatic betrayal are central themes during the exam, alongside clear assistive device reluctance evident by a brand-new walking frame pushed into a corner.
Environmental Observations: Trip hazards present; assistive devices are visibly hidden away.
Medication Reconciliation: No acute changes to regular chronic illness medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Chronic Illness / Disability Identity Grief - Prolonged Grief Disorder (or Adjustment Disorder) - V62.89 (Phase of life problem) - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Acute Myocardial Infarction - Ruled out/unlikely due to absence of chest pain, shortness of breath, or typical ischemic symptoms, with the crash directly linked to recognized extreme physical over-exertion.

Adrenal Insufficiency Crisis - Ruled out/unlikely due to the absence of vomiting, severe abdominal pain, or significant electrolyte-associated confusion, though mild hypotension is present.

Stroke/TIA - Ruled out/unlikely due to bilateral, symmetrical weakness without focal neurological deficits or facial droop.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with acute, severe physical collapse, mild hypotension (BP 95/60), and reactive tachycardia (HR 108) following a massive physical over-exertion event driven by underlying psychological grief and stigma. Urgent, out-of-hours face-to-face assessment was required to definitively differentiate this profound post-exertional malaise from an acute ischemic event, adrenal crisis, or severe metabolic derangement. Delaying care until routine morning hours carried an unacceptable risk of missing a life-threatening complication of their chronic illness or facilitating a severe fall due to profound weakness, strictly meeting the criteria for urgent medical intervention.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Initiated strict bed rest and oral fluid resuscitation to correct mild hypotension. Advised usual GP to arrange a narrative assessment highlighting disrupted autobiographical memory integration and to review systemic markers.
Positive Behavioral Change: To mitigate post-exertional crashes, implement a strict 'pacing' protocol, limiting physical activity to 50% of perceived capacity; logging these successful, symptom-free periods will positively reinforce the value of energy conservation. To overcome assistive device reluctance, commit to using the mobility aid for just 5 minutes inside the house daily; successfully completing this low-stakes task will normalize the device and gradually extinguish the internalized stigma.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Referral to a specialized chronic illness occupational therapist and grief counselor.
DME/Safety (Optional): Encourage use of the already-purchased walking frame.
Follow-up: Routine GP review within 48 hours to ensure physical recovery from the crash and to initiate psychological support referrals.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate presentation to the Emergency Department or contact 000 if experiencing sudden chest pain, loss of consciousness, inability to pass urine for more than 8 hours, or new focal neurological deficits.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is presumptive pending investigations; conditions are dynamic and require ongoing review.
Clinical Course: The acute physical crash will require 3 to 7 days of aggressive rest and pacing to return to baseline. The psychological grief process is long-term and requires structured, professional support.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Call 000 immediately if you experience chest pain, faint and lose consciousness, or suddenly cannot move one side of your body. Contact your regular GP if you are still completely unable to get out of bed after 48 hours of rest.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Your body is experiencing a severe energy deficit because you pushed past its current physical limits. The low blood pressure and fast heart rate are signs of this exhaustion, and complete rest is the only immediate treatment.
Actionable Steps: Drink plenty of fluids today to support your blood pressure. Use your walking frame for all indoor movements over the next 48 hours to prevent a fall while your muscles recover.

